const projects={
  "radar": {
    "label": "RESEARCH / LAWRENCE BERKELEY NATIONAL LABORATORY",
    "title": "Atmospheric radar research at Berkeley Lab",
    "technical": `<p class="detail-lead">Repurposing a TI AWR2944 77–81 GHz automotive FMCW radar into a low-power atmospheric sensor: configurable waveforms, autonomous acquisition, custom TLV processing, weak-return enhancement, and remote deployment.</p>
<div class="radar-case-map"><a href="#radar-problem"><span>01</span><strong>Sensing problem</strong><small>Why atmospheric returns are difficult</small></a><a href="#radar-front-end"><span>02</span><strong>Radar front end</strong><small>Waveforms, range, Doppler, resolution</small></a><a href="#radar-control"><span>03</span><strong>Autonomous control</strong><small>Pi, Feather, RTC, relay, storage</small></a><a href="#radar-dsp"><span>04</span><strong>Acquisition & data pipeline</strong><small>Configuration, capture, processing</small></a><a href="#radar-weak"><span>05</span><strong>Reflector & detection</strong><small>Long-range test, RCS, CFAR</small></a><a href="#radar-field"><span>06</span><strong>Field system</strong><small>Power, reliability, deployment</small></a></div>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-problem"><p class="chapter-number">01 / THE SENSING PROBLEM</p><h3>Atmospheric sensing goals</h3><p class="radar-problem-intro">Our goal is to adapt automotive mmWave radar for frequent, distributed observations of precipitation and other processes in the atmospheric boundary layer.</p><div class="radar-problem-grid"><div class="radar-problem-panel radar-platform-choice"><h4>Why automotive radar?</h4><p><b>Low cost and low power consumption</b> make these compact sensors attractive for deploying multiple instruments and supporting long field experiments with a limited power budget.</p></div><div class="radar-problem-panel"><h4>The sensing challenge</h4><p>Atmospheric returns can be much weaker than vehicle reflections. Detecting them requires separating faint echoes from receiver noise and stationary ground or structural clutter.</p></div></div><div class="radar-project-focus"><p class="radar-focus-label">MY ENGINEERING FOCUS</p><p>Configure the radar, improve weak-return processing, and build reliable power and acquisition control for unattended measurements.</p></div></section>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-front-end"><p class="chapter-number">02 / FMCW FOUNDATIONS</p><h3>FMCW radar fundamentals</h3><p>In the months before joining the research team, I studied mmWave radar and FMCW signal processing. The figures below summarize the signal chain, range and velocity estimation, and measurement limits that formed the foundation for my contributions to radar configuration and data processing.</p><div class="radar-theory-gallery" aria-label="FMCW radar signal chain and measurement theory"><figure class="detail-figure radar-theory-chain"><a class="theory-image-link" href="assets/radar-fmcw-signal-chain.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the FMCW signal-chain diagram at full size"><img src="assets/radar-fmcw-signal-chain.svg" alt="FMCW transmitter: ramp generator, local oscillator, power amplifier, and TX antenna. Receiver: RX antenna, low-noise amplifier, mixer, IF stage, ADC, and DSP." width="1200" height="510" loading="lazy"><span>Open full size ↗</span></a><figcaption><b>How FMCW radar works.</b> Transmitting, receiving, and processing the radar signal.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure"><a class="theory-image-link" href="assets/radar-fmcw-mixing.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the FMCW beat-frequency and chirp slide at full size"><img src="assets/radar-fmcw-mixing-preview.webp" alt="Lecture slide showing signed beat-frequency equations for up and down chirps and the relationships to target range and Doppler shift." width="960" height="540" loading="lazy"><span>Open full size ↗</span></a><figcaption><b>Calculating range and velocity.</b> Using the transmitted and received chirps to find distance and motion.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure"><a class="theory-image-link" href="assets/radar-fmcw-limits.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the FMCW range and velocity resolution slide at full size"><img src="assets/radar-fmcw-limits-preview.webp" alt="Lecture slide relating range resolution to bandwidth and velocity resolution to wavelength, chirp count, and chirp repetition period." width="960" height="540" loading="lazy"><span>Open full size ↗</span></a><figcaption><b>Range and velocity limits.</b> Calculating resolution and maximum measurable range and velocity.</figcaption></figure></div></section>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-control"><p class="chapter-number">03 / AUTONOMOUS HARDWARE & POWER</p><h3>Autonomous control hardware</h3><p>The Feather microcontroller manages when the radar system runs. It uses a FeatherWing real-time clock to keep the measurement schedule, then switches on the Raspberry Pi and radar through a latching relay for each acquisition window. The Pi configures the radar and records its data; between measurement windows, the Feather can switch those components off to save power. Because the Feather operates independently of the Pi, it can also cycle their power during recovery if the Pi stalls.</p><h3>A compact Adafruit/Raspberry Pi assembly</h3><p>I integrated an Adafruit MCU and its peripherals with the Raspberry Pi using an Adafruit/Raspberry Pi HAT. Stacking the boards keeps the control hardware and its connections together in a compact assembly.</p><div class="radar-integration-pair"><figure class="detail-figure integration-photo"><a href="assets/adafruit-raspi-stack-portrait.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="assets/adafruit-raspi-stack-portrait.svg" alt="Adafruit MCU and peripheral boards stacked compactly with a Raspberry Pi through an Adafruit/Raspberry Pi HAT" width="1500" height="2000" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Adafruit MCU and peripherals integrated with the Raspberry Pi in a compact HAT-based stack.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure detail-video integration-video"><video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="assets/adafruit-peripherals-poster.jpg" aria-label="Silent portrait video of Hashem working on Adafruit peripheral connections"><source src="assets/adafruit-peripherals.mp4" type="video/mp4">Your browser does not support embedded video.</video><figcaption><span>Working on the Adafruit peripheral connections.</span><button type="button" class="text-link" data-video-toggle>Play video</button></figcaption></figure></div><section class="radar-power-section" aria-labelledby="radar-power-title"><h3 id="radar-power-title">Relay control and regulated power</h3><div class="radar-power-layout"><div class="radar-power-copy"><p>The controller switches power independently of the Raspberry Pi, managing scheduled wake windows and restarting the acquisition hardware when needed.</p><p>I use DC–DC buck converters to step down and regulate the supply. When the relay reconnects the radar, they provide stable operating voltages for startup and data acquisition.</p><div class="radar-power-sequence" aria-label="Power sequence"><span>Scheduled wake</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Relay on</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Regulated power</span></div></div><div class="radar-power-media"><figure class="detail-figure detail-video relay-demo"><video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="assets/relay-demo-poster.jpg" aria-label="Silent bench video of the relay power-control prototype"><source src="assets/relay-demo.mp4" type="video/mp4">Your browser does not support embedded video.</video><figcaption><span>Relay switching prototype.</span><button type="button" class="text-link" data-video-toggle>Play video</button></figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure radar-power-figure"><a href="assets/radar-regulated-power.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="assets/radar-regulated-power.jpg" alt="DC–DC converters wired to the relay-controlled Raspberry Pi and Adafruit controller assembly" width="2361" height="2333" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>DC–DC converters and regulated power wiring.</figcaption></figure></div></div></section></section>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-dsp"><p class="chapter-number">04 / ACQUISITION, PROCESSING & PACKETIZATION</p><h3>Radar control and data acquisition</h3><p>The Raspberry Pi does more than collect serial bytes. At boot it opens separate CLI and data UART paths, discovers configuration files, sends the selected command sequence to the radar, starts and stops each acquisition, names and records the binary stream, copies the configuration beside the measurement, and writes status logs. Keeping the exact configuration with the data makes each run traceable.</p><div class="code-evidence-grid"><figure class="detail-figure"><div class="radar-code-crop"><img src="assets/radar-pi-code.jpg" alt="Raspberry Pi Python acquisition code showing configuration upload, sensor start, serial data recording, and sensor stop" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>For each radar system, the Raspberry Pi cycles through multiple DSP configurations within one scheduled measurement window, recording each configuration separately so their results can be compared.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure"><img src="assets/radar-run-log.jpg" alt="Raspberry Pi run log showing sensor stop, stored data size, and experiment end time" loading="lazy"><figcaption>End-of-run logging records the stop command, 1,320,000-byte capture, and completion timestamp.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Radar data processing and packetization</h3><div class="radar-cube-layout"><figure class="detail-figure radar-cube-figure"><a class="theory-image-link" href="assets/radar-cube-simple.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the radar cube figure at full size"><img src="assets/radar-cube-simple.svg" alt="Radar cube with range, Doppler, and channel dimensions. Samples within each chirp support range processing; successive chirps support velocity processing; antenna channels support direction estimation." width="1200" height="760" loading="lazy"><span>Open full size ↗</span></a><figcaption>Organizing radar data by samples, chirps, and antenna channels.</figcaption></figure><div class="radar-cube-copy"><h4>Reading the radar cube</h4><p>Samples, chirps, and antenna channels organize each frame. <b>Range FFTs</b> resolve distance, <b>Doppler FFTs</b> resolve radial velocity, and phase differences between antennas support direction estimates.</p><h4>Parsing radar output</h4><p>My Python parser validates the radar’s <b>TLV packets</b> and converts their processed outputs into NumPy arrays for range–Doppler maps and range or noise profiles.</p><h4>Mapping measurements in space</h4><p>I package recordings with their <b>radar ID, configuration, reference tower, coordinates, and orientation</b>. This metadata links each range bin to its location along the radar beam, identifying the part of the atmosphere sampled.</p></div></div><div class="pipeline-diagram radar-cube-pipeline" aria-label="Radar data output pipeline"><span>Configure + capture</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Radar processing</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>TLV parsing</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Packetization + metadata</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Spatial mapping</span></div></section>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-weak"><p class="chapter-number">05 / REFLECTOR TEST & DETECTION</p><section class="reflector-experiment" aria-labelledby="reflector-test-title"><p class="eyebrow">LONG-RANGE REFLECTOR TEST</p><h4 id="reflector-test-title">Long-range reflector test</h4><p>We tested <strong>two spinning reflectors 175 m from the TI AWR2944 radar</strong>: one with a strong radar cross section (RCS), and one with a much weaker RCS. This lets us compare long-range visibility for strong and weak radar targets. The paired animations show the outdoor setup and the corresponding range–Doppler display.</p><div class="reflector-animation-group" data-gif-pair><div class="reflector-animations"><figure class="detail-figure"><img src="assets/reflector-setup-still.jpg" data-gif-src="assets/reflector-setup.gif" data-still-src="assets/reflector-setup-still.jpg" alt="Outdoor spinning-corner-reflector test setup" width="319" height="568" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Outdoor reflector setup.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure"><img src="assets/reflector-display-still.jpg" data-gif-src="assets/reflector-display.gif" data-still-src="assets/reflector-display-still.jpg" alt="Range–Doppler display during the spinning-reflector experiment" width="319" height="568" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Inspecting the range–Doppler return.</figcaption></figure></div><button type="button" class="text-link" data-gif-toggle aria-pressed="true">Play animations</button></div><div class="reflector-rcs"><h5>How reflective is a 5 cm corner?</h5><p>For an ideal conducting <strong>square trihedral</strong> with interior edge <em>a</em> = 0.05 m, aligned for peak return:</p><p class="reflector-equation">λ = c/f &nbsp; · &nbsp; σ<sub>peak</sub> ≈ 12πa<sup>4</sup>/λ<sup>2</sup></p><p>At the 79 GHz band center, λ ≈ <strong>3.795 mm</strong>, giving σ<sub>peak</sub> ≈ <strong>16.36 m² (12.14 dBsm)</strong>. Across 77–81 GHz, the estimate ranges from <strong>15.54 to 17.20 m²</strong> (11.92–12.36 dBsm).</p><p class="fine">This is a calculated peak RCS. The return varies as the reflector spins; alignment, construction, and ground reflections affect the observed response. The 175 m separation is the test distance.</p><p class="reference-line">References: <a href="https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc902372/m2/1/high_res_d/929123.pdf#page=12" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sandia · square-trihedral model, Eq. 4 ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.ti.com/product/AWR2944" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TI AWR2944 ↗</a></p></div></section><section class="radar-cfar-analysis" id="radar-cfar" aria-labelledby="radar-cfar-title"><p class="eyebrow">CFAR & DETECTION</p><h4 id="radar-cfar-title">CFAR detection</h4><p>I use CFAR (constant false alarm rate) to estimate the local noise floor around regions of interest in the range–Doppler map. Nearby reference cells provide the background estimate, while guard cells help keep the target’s return out of that estimate. This sets an adaptive detection threshold that follows the local background, helping distinguish potential target returns from noise.</p><div class="radar-cfar-pair"><figure class="detail-figure"><a href="assets/radar-cfar-original.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the original range–Doppler power map at full size"><img src="assets/radar-cfar-original.png" alt="Original range–Doppler power heatmap before CFAR detection" width="924" height="390" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><b>Before CFAR.</b> The spinning reflector presents a larger radar cross section (RCS) from the front and a smaller RCS from the back, producing stronger and weaker returns in the range–Doppler map. <a href="assets/radar-cfar-original.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full size ↗</a></figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure"><a href="assets/radar-cfar-detection.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Open the binary CFAR detection mask at full size"><img src="assets/radar-cfar-detection.svg" alt="CFAR range–Doppler detection mask with a Detection color scale labeled 0 and 1" width="920" height="390" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><b>After CFAR.</b> The binary mask highlights returns above the local threshold, including the reflector’s stronger front-face and weaker back-face returns. <a href="assets/radar-cfar-detection.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full size ↗</a></figcaption></figure></div><figure class="detail-figure radar-cfar-code"><div class="radar-cfar-crop"><img src="assets/radar-cfar-workbench.jpg" alt="Cropped notebook workstation view showing original heatmap, background estimate, and detection mask beside Python CFAR code" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption><b>The implementation.</b> The Python CFAR code sits beside its diagnostic plots, making it easier to inspect how the background estimate leads to the detection mask.</figcaption></figure></section></section>
<section class="radar-chapter" id="radar-field"><p class="chapter-number">06 / INTEGRATION, POWER & FIELD DEPLOYMENT</p><h3>Hardware integration and field deployment</h3><p>The integrated enclosure combines the radar, conductive-PLA parabolic reflector, Raspberry Pi, Feather controller, relay, external storage, and regulated power stages. The reflector narrows the effective field of view for beam-focused measurements. The controller coordinates power sequencing and wake windows; the Pi manages experiment state, radar configuration, data files, and logs.</p><figure class="detail-figure radar-wide"><img src="assets/radar-integrated-1083.jpg" alt="Integrated atmospheric radar enclosure with reflector, radar board, Raspberry Pi, and control electronics" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Integrated radar, reflector, compute, control, and power distribution.</figcaption></figure><div class="result-pair three"><div><strong>~5.3 W</strong><span>reported average system power</span></div><div><strong>1.37 GB/day</strong><span>reported data rate per radar</span></div><div><strong>Up to 6 months</strong><span>estimated 256 GB recording duration</span></div></div><p>Since July 1, 2026, all ten radar systems have been running reliably at Jornada Experimental Range, New Mexico. The deployment brings together scheduled acquisition, automated power control, and remote monitoring to support long-term atmospheric observations.</p><div class="radar-deployment-milestone"><div><strong>10</strong><span>radars operating reliably</span></div><div><strong data-deployment-days>136</strong><span>days since deployment</span></div><p>Deployed July 1, 2026 · Updated daily</p></div><figure class="detail-figure radar-rainfall"><a href="assets/radar-rainfall.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="assets/radar-rainfall.svg" alt="Radar return power over time and range, with stronger returns during rainfall" width="1400" height="560" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><b>Detecting rainfall.</b> Return power from a vertically pointing radar, shown over time and range along the zenith-directed beam. Stronger returns indicate rainfall in the atmospheric column overhead. <a href="assets/radar-rainfall.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full size ↗</a></figcaption></figure><div class="radar-field-gallery"><figure class="detail-figure"><img src="assets/field.webp" alt="Atmospheric radar system deployed at Jornada Experimental Range in New Mexico" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Meteorological tower and field instruments at Jornada Experimental Range, New Mexico.</figcaption></figure><figure class="detail-figure casis-event-photo"><a href="assets/casis-event.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="assets/casis-event.jpg" alt="Group photograph of participants at a CASIS event" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>CASIS event group photo.</figcaption></figure></div><a class="button" href="assets/research-poster.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the CASIS poster ↗</a></section>`,
    "storyTitle": "My time at LBNL",
    "story": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I joined Berkeley Lab in January 2026 and have spent roughly 10 months working with my mentor, Stijn Wielandt.</p><h3>From internships to an ongoing role</h3><p>During that time, I completed two 10-week, full-time internships and continued as an undergraduate research assistant between those periods. I currently work under Stijn’s supervision alongside my studies at UC Berkeley.</p><p>Returning to the same research over several parts of the year has given me time to stay involved as the radar system develops. My experience has moved between software, hardware integration, and outdoor testing, with Stijn’s mentorship connecting those parts of the work.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/lab-portrait.jpg?v=8651\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/lab-portrait.jpg?v=8651\" alt=\"Hashem with radar hardware at Berkeley Lab\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Working with the radar system during my time at Berkeley Lab.</figcaption></figure><h3>Outdoor validation</h3><p>This nighttime test brought the full measurement chain outdoors: the tripod-mounted radar, live laptop interface, configuration workflow, and operator checks all ran together in the intended sensing geometry.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/outdoor-testing.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/outdoor-testing.jpg\" alt=\"Hashem operating a tripod-mounted atmospheric radar with a laptop during a nighttime outdoor test at Berkeley Lab\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Nighttime end-to-end radar validation on the Berkeley Lab hillside.</figcaption></figure><h3>Visiting the Advanced Light Source</h3><p>A visit to Berkeley Lab’s Advanced Light Source offered a closer look at another large-scale scientific instrument and the people and infrastructure behind research across the laboratory.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/lbnl-als-visit.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/lbnl-als-visit.jpg\" alt=\"Group visit inside Berkeley Lab’s Advanced Light Source facility\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Visiting the Advanced Light Source with fellow Berkeley Lab researchers and students.</figcaption></figure><h3>Photos from Berkeley Lab</h3><p>The photos capture a few parts of those months: working with the equipment, testing outdoors, visiting the ALS, meeting Mike Witherell, then Berkeley Lab’s director, and the view across the bay. Alongside the technical project, they give a sense of the place where I have spent much of this year.</p><div class=\"detail-photo-pair lab-memories-gallery\"><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a class=\"lab-group-crop\" href=\"assets/lbnl-als-visit.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/lbnl-als-visit.jpg\" alt=\"Group visit at Berkeley Lab\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>A group visit at Berkeley Lab.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/lbnl-director.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/lbnl-director.jpg\" alt=\"Hashem with Mike Witherell, then Berkeley Lab director, and a colleague\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>With Mike Witherell, then director of Berkeley Lab.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/sunset.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/sunset.jpg\" alt=\"View across the Bay Area from the laboratory\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>A view across the bay from the lab.</figcaption></figure></div>"
  },
  "inverter": {
    "label": "POWER ELECTRONICS / JUL–AUG 2025",
    "title": "Discrete H-bridge inverter",
    "technical": `<p class="detail-lead">A breadboarded DC-to-AC inverter using IRF4905 PMOS and IRFZ44N NMOS devices, discrete BJT control, and microcontroller timing.</p>
<div class="inverter-hero-grid">
  <figure class="detail-figure detail-video inverter-demo"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="assets/inverter-demo-poster.jpg" aria-label="Inverter powering anti-parallel red and blue LEDs"><source src="assets/inverter-demo.mp4" type="video/mp4">Your browser does not support embedded video.</video><figcaption><span>Proof of operation: the opposing LEDs respond to opposite output polarities.</span><button class="text-link" type="button" data-video-toggle>Pause video</button></figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="detail-figure schematic-feature"><a href="assets/inverter-final-schematic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="assets/inverter-final-schematic.jpg" alt="LTspice H-bridge schematic with two NMOS driver capacitors and a 16 V supply" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Topology reference: two NMOS driver capacitors. This simulation screenshot uses a 16 V supply.</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<h3>Inverter testing</h3><p>The anti-parallel red and blue LEDs provide a direct polarity check: one is forward-biased for one bridge polarity and the other for the reverse polarity. This establishes a bipolar output at the load; it does not alone establish a sinusoidal waveform, RMS voltage, current capacity, or efficiency.</p>
<h3>Revised switching logic</h3><div class="detail-skills"><div><h4>High-side PMOS</h4><p>Pulling the gate toward ground makes V<sub>GS</sub> negative and turns the device on. Returning the gate to its source turns it off.</p></div><div><h4>Low-side NMOS</h4><p>A positive V<sub>GS</sub> turns the device on. The revised topology retains the two NMOS capacitor gate-driver circuits.</p></div><div><h4>Complementary leg drive</h4><p>Each leg commands the PMOS and NMOS oppositely, reducing simultaneous conduction through the rail.</p></div><div><h4>Output reversal</h4><p>Alternating diagonal device pairs reverses the voltage across the load.</p></div></div>
<p class="engineering-note"><strong>Design caveat.</strong> Complementary commands reduce shoot-through risk, but real hardware still needs dead-time and oscilloscope verification because device and driver delays are unequal.</p>
`,
    "storyTitle": "From four gate drivers to the 20 V prototype",
    "story": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I started with a separate capacitor gate-driver circuit for each MOSFET, then simplified the PMOS drive when I chose a 20 V input.</p>\n<div class=\"iteration-list\">\n<article><span>01 / FIRST PROTOTYPE</span><div><h3>One gate driver per MOSFET</h3><p>The first H-bridge had four capacitor gate-driver circuits: one for each of the two PMOS and two NMOS devices.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><img src=\"assets/inverter-four-cap.jpg\" alt=\"First breadboard H-bridge with four capacitor gate-driver circuits, one per MOSFET\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>First prototype: four MOSFETs and four gate-driver capacitors.</figcaption></figure></div></article>\n<article><span>02 / 20 V INPUT</span><div><h3>Simplifying the PMOS gate drive</h3><p>When I decided to use a 20 V input, I removed the capacitor gate-driver setup for the two PMOS devices. I also removed the other two BJTs to improve efficiency. The revised PMOS drive pulls each gate toward ground to turn it on and returns it toward its source to turn it off. The two NMOS capacitor gate-driver circuits remained.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><img src=\"assets/inverter-two-cap.jpg\" alt=\"Revised breadboard H-bridge with two gate-driver capacitors remaining\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Revised prototype: PMOS capacitor gate drivers removed; two NMOS driver capacitors retained.</figcaption></figure></div></article>\n<article><span>03 / LAST HARDWARE ITERATION</span><div><h3>A 16 V capacitor at 20 V</h3><p>In the last hardware iteration, I applied 20 V across a capacitor rated for 16 V. That exceeded its voltage rating and caused it to break down. The photo shows the H-bridge afterward, with one intact gate-driver capacitor remaining.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><img src=\"assets/inverter-failure.jpg\" alt=\"Last breadboard H-bridge iteration after capacitor breakdown, with one intact electrolytic gate-driver capacitor remaining\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Last hardware iteration, after a 16 V capacitor broke down under 20 V.</figcaption></figure></div></article>\n</div>"
  },
  "oscilloscope": {
    "label": "PCB DESIGN & FPGA ACQUISITION / IN PROGRESS",
    "title": "Building Scopii",
    "technical": "<div class=\"scopii-interactive\"><iframe allowfullscreen=\"\" height=\"720\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://personal-viewer.365.altium.com/client/index.html?feature=embed&amp;source=92155BB1-210F-4413-8E50-1EC63BB96544&amp;activeView=3D\" title=\"Scopii interactive PCB and 3D viewer\" width=\"1280\"></iframe></div><p class=\"fine\"><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"assets/oscilloscope-pcb-clean.png\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">View board image ↗</a></p><p class=\"detail-lead\">Our team at UC Berkeley is building Scopii—the name we chose for our two-channel, PC-connected oscilloscope. Its six-layer board brings together the probe circuitry, high-speed ADC, FPGA, and USB interface to turn analog inputs into waveform data for a PC.</p><h3>From ADC to waveform display</h3><p>I’m responsible for the acquisition and display path, starting with ADC selection and configuration and continuing through FPGA capture, sample buffering, packetization, and USB transfer. The next stage is the PC application that will display waveforms and send acquisition settings back to the instrument.</p><div class=\"detail-skills\"><div><h4>ADC capture</h4><p>Receive the ADS4229’s two 12-bit channels over source-synchronous LVDS DDR connections, using the forwarded data clock to capture the incoming samples.</p></div><div><h4>Data routing &amp; packetization</h4><p>Organize the captured channel data in the ECP5 and assemble it into packets for the host transfer path.</p></div><div><h4>FTDI streaming</h4><p>Move the packetized data onto the FT601Q’s 32-bit parallel interface and onward over USB 3.0 to a PC.</p></div><div><h4>Design specifications</h4><p>Two synchronized 12-bit ADC channels, a 250 MHz sampling clock, and an ECP5 LFE5U-25F FPGA. These describe the design; bring-up and measured performance validation remain ahead.</p></div></div><div class=\"scopii-document-links\"><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"assets/scopii-schematics.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Full schematics (PDF) ↗</a><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"assets/scopii-pcb-layout.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">PCB layout (PDF) ↗</a></div><h3>System architecture</h3><figure class=\"detail-figure oscilloscope-diagram\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-architecture.png\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Original oscilloscope block diagram from analog inputs through ADC, FPGA and FTDI to a computer\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-architecture.png\"/></a><figcaption>System architecture · Analog conditioning → ADC → FPGA → FTDI → PC</figcaption></figure><h3>Six-layer PCB layout</h3><p>The board connects two analog input channels to the ADS4229 converter, then routes its differential outputs to the ECP5 FPGA. A parallel interface links the FPGA to the FT601Q USB bridge. These layers show the component interconnects, power distribution, and routing that bring the acquisition chain together.</p><div class=\"scope-layout-gallery\"><figure class=\"detail-figure\" data-layer=\"L1\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l1-reference-centered.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"L1 · Top signal routing\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l1-reference-centered.svg\"/></a><figcaption>L1 · Top signal routing</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\" data-layer=\"L2\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l2-reference-centered.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"L2 · Ground plane\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l2-reference-centered.svg\"/></a><figcaption>L2 · Ground plane</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\" data-layer=\"L3\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l3-reference-centered.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"L3 · Internal signal routing\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l3-reference-centered.svg\"/></a><figcaption>L3 · Internal signal routing</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\" data-layer=\"L4\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l4-reference-centered.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"L4 · Ground plane\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l4-reference-centered.svg\"/></a><figcaption>L4 · Ground plane</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\" data-layer=\"L5\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l5-reference-centered.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"L5 · Power distribution and polygon pours\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l5-reference-centered.svg\"/></a><figcaption>L5 · Power distribution and polygon pours</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\" data-layer=\"L6\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l6-reference-centered.svg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"L6 · Bottom signal routing\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-layer-l6-reference-centered.svg\"/></a><figcaption>L6 · Bottom signal routing</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Conversion, capture, and host transfer</h3><p>The schematic sections connect the analog input chain to FPGA acquisition and USB streaming. My work focuses on capturing the converter output, organizing the samples in memory, and framing the data for transfer to a PC.</p><section class=\"scope-schematic\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">01 / ANALOG SIGNAL CONDITIONING</p><h3>Analog front end · Preparing the probe signal</h3><p>The OPA140 and BUF802 work together to buffer the incoming probe signal, combining accurate low-frequency response with high-bandwidth performance. Their high input impedance reduces the load placed on the circuit being measured, while their low output impedance allows the signal to drive the following amplification and filtering stages without significant degradation.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-analog-driver.png\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Analog front end · Differential ADC drive\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-analog-driver.webp\"/></a><figcaption>Analog front end · Schematic sheet 4 · Open full resolution ↗</figcaption></figure></section><section class=\"scope-schematic\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">02 / ANALOG-TO-DIGITAL CONVERSION</p><h3>ADS4229 · Two-channel, 12-bit acquisition</h3><p>The ADS4229 digitizes both input channels at up to 250 MSPS with 12-bit resolution. An external SiT9365 provides the ADC’s 250 MHz differential sampling clock. This low-jitter MEMS oscillator uses temperature sensing and compensation to stabilize its frequency, with complementary LVDS outputs driving the ADC’s clock inputs. The ADC sends each channel’s data to the FPGA over six parallel LVDS pairs using double-data-rate (DDR) signaling. A forwarded data clock provides the timing reference for capturing both edges.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-adc-clock.png\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"ADS4229 · Sampling and LVDS output\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-adc-clock.webp\"/></a><figcaption>ADS4229 · Schematic sheet 3 · Open full resolution ↗</figcaption></figure></section><section class=\"scope-schematic\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">03 / FPGA CAPTURE &amp; PACKETIZATION</p><h3>FPGA banks 2 &amp; 3 · Capture, buffering, and packetization</h3><p>Banks 2 and 3 receive the ADC’s LVDS data and forwarded clock. I’m developing the FPGA logic to capture data on both clock edges, reconstruct the 12-bit samples, and pair the channels in embedded RAM. The packetizer then groups buffered sample pairs, adds a header, and passes each frame to the FTDI interface.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-fpga-lvds.png\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"ECP5 FPGA · Source-synchronous ADC capture\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-fpga-lvds.webp\"/></a><figcaption>FPGA banks 2 &amp; 3 · Schematic sheet 9 · Open full resolution ↗</figcaption></figure></section><section class=\"scope-schematic\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">04 / FPGA-TO-FTDI INTERFACE</p><h3>FPGA banks 6 &amp; 7 · Streaming framed samples</h3><p>Banks 6 and 7 connect the ECP5 to the FT601Q through a 32-bit, single-ended LVCMOS interface. The data bus carries packetized sample frames, while the FIFO clock, byte enables, and handshake signals coordinate transfers. This is the output side of my FPGA data path: moving buffered acquisition data into the USB bridge as the interface accepts it.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-fpga-ftdi.png\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"ECP5 FPGA · Packetized data to the host interface\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-fpga-ftdi.webp\"/></a><figcaption>FPGA banks 6 &amp; 7 · Schematic sheet 12 · Open full resolution ↗</figcaption></figure></section><section class=\"scope-schematic\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">05 / USB STREAMING &amp; HOST CONNECTION</p><h3>FT601Q · USB 3.0 connection to the PC</h3><p>The FT601Q forms the final stage of the digital acquisition path, bridging a 32-bit parallel FIFO interface to USB 3.0 through the board’s USB-C connection. Its external 30 MHz crystal provides the reference for internal clock generation, and the USB connection also supplies board power. At a 100 MHz FIFO clock, the 32-bit interface has a maximum transfer rate of 400 MB/s; achieved PC throughput depends on the host and transfer implementation. This is an interface specification, not a measured result for our board.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure schematic-sheet\"><a href=\"assets/oscilloscope-usb-ftdi.png\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"FT601Q · USB streaming\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/oscilloscope-usb-ftdi.webp\"/></a><figcaption>FT601Q · Schematic sheet 16 · Open full resolution ↗</figcaption></figure></section>",
    "storyTitle": "Building the instrument, end to end",
    "story": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">This team project is part of the IEEE-run Advanced PCB Engineering DeCal at UC Berkeley. It brings together board design, FPGA development, and PC software in a single measurement instrument.</p><h3>Owning the acquisition and display path</h3><p>My responsibility runs from the ADC to the waveform on the PC. I’m involved in selecting, configuring, and interfacing the ADC, and I own the FPGA capture, buffering, packetization, and FTDI transfer path. That responsibility also extends to the host software that will turn the incoming samples into an interactive waveform display.</p><h3>Learning through the APE DeCal</h3><p>Building Scopii is teaching me to think at the system level: how the probe input, analog conditioning, ADC, FPGA, USB interface, and waveform display work together as a measurement instrument. Beyond designing my own circuitry and acquisition logic, I’m learning how decisions across these stages affect signal integrity, bandwidth, noise, and reliable operation.</p><p>Through the APE DeCal, I’m developing practical experience with Altium schematics and PCB layout while collaborating on a shared design. The project makes mixed-signal engineering tangible: a circuit choice can change what reaches the ADC, clocking and routing affect sample capture, and buffering and transfer limits shape what the PC can display. Understanding those dependencies helps me evaluate my work against the performance of the complete instrument.</p><h3>Next: waveform display and instrument control</h3><p>I’ll build the PC application to receive sample frames over USB, display the waveforms, and send configuration commands back to the board. Planned controls include trigger settings and other acquisition options. Board bring-up and end-to-end validation are still ahead.</p><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"https://ape.berkie.ee/\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Explore the APE DeCal ↗</a>"
},
  "cpu": {
    "label": "COMPUTER ARCHITECTURE / IN PROGRESS",
    "title": "32-bit RISC-V CPU",
    "technical": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">A 32-bit RISC-V processor I’m developing in Logisim-evolution for CS 61C at UC Berkeley.</p><p>The design connects an ALU, register file, immediate generator, control logic, branch comparator, and load/store blocks. Instruction execution and verification are still in progress.</p><h3>Processor architecture</h3><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><img alt=\"CPU architecture overview connecting instruction fields, control logic, registers, ALU, branch comparison, and memory access\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/cpu-overview.svg\"/><figcaption>Architecture overview of the current source blocks. Earlier Logisim screenshots above document the initial integration.</figcaption></figure><h3>Arithmetic logic unit</h3><p>32-bit arithmetic, logic, shifts, comparisons, and multiplication, selected through a common result multiplexer.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/cpu-alu.png\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Logisim ALU circuit with arithmetic, bitwise, shift, compare, and multiply paths\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/cpu-alu.png\"/></a><figcaption>Logisim schematic · Click to enlarge</figcaption></figure><h3>Register file</h3><p>32 registers with two read ports, clocked write-back, and inspection outputs for debugging.</p><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/cpu-register-file.png\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Register file with write-selection decoder, dual read multiplexers, and debug outputs\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/cpu-register-file.png\"/></a><figcaption>Logisim schematic · Click to enlarge</figcaption></figure>",
    "storyTitle": "Learning computer architecture",
    "story": "<p>I’m building the processor’s component circuits and integrating them into a CPU datapath. Working in Logisim lets me trace how instructions move through registers, control logic, and the ALU, connecting the architecture I study in CS 61C to signals I can inspect and debug.</p>"
},
  "fusion": {
    "label": "EXPERIMENTAL PHYSICS / IN PROGRESS",
    "title": "IEC plasma confinement project",
    "technical": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">An experimental inertial electrostatic confinement device in development, bringing together vacuum-system integration and electrode design.</p><h3>Tools and skills</h3><div class=\"detail-skills\"><div><h4>System integration</h4><p>Vacuum chamber layout, electrode arrangement, and dual-stage pumping.</p></div><div><h4>Experimental design</h4><p>Low-pressure operating conditions and electrostatic ion-acceleration geometry.</p></div></div><h3>Design scope</h3><p>The planned arrangement uses a spherical inner electrode grid at −6 kV inside a grounded outer electrode and vacuum chamber. A dual-stage pumping system is being integrated to establish low-pressure conditions for plasma experiments and electrostatic ion acceleration.</p><h3>Current status</h3><p>In progress. The current materials describe development and integration; measured fusion performance is a future milestone.</p>",
    "storyTitle": "A curiosity about fusion",
    "story": "<div class=\"iec-notebook\"><section class=\"iec-journal-intro\"><h3>Why I started this</h3><p>I started this project independently because I’m fascinated by physics and how matter behaves at high energies. I wanted to move beyond studying the theory and explore the conditions required for nuclear fusion through hands-on experimentation.</p></section><section class=\"iec-journal-physics\"><h3>The physics I’m curious about</h3><div class=\"iec-observations\"><article><h4><span aria-hidden=\"true\">↓</span> Chamber pressure</h4><p>Reducing pressure increases the ions’ mean free path, allowing them to travel farther between collisions. I’m interested in how that changes their path through the chamber.</p></article><article><h4><span aria-hidden=\"true\">↑</span> Applied voltage</h4><p>A greater accelerating potential lets ions gain more kinetic energy, increasing their ability to overcome electrostatic repulsion between nuclei.</p></article></div><p class=\"iec-journal-question\">I’m exploring how these two parameters interact in establishing the conditions needed for fusion.</p></section><section class=\"iec-journal-setup\"><h3>Equipment and setup</h3><table class=\"iec-equipment-table\" aria-label=\"IEC experimental equipment\"><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">High-voltage supply</th><td>12 kV AC</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Vacuum system</th><td>Dual-stage pumping · approximately 3 Pa minimum pressure achieved</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Enclosure</th><td>1/2-inch polycarbonate</td></tr></tbody></table></section><p class=\"iec-journal-progress\"><span>Work in progress</span> I’m still developing the setup and learning from the process. I haven’t demonstrated fusion.</p></div>"
},
  "bytes": {
    "label": "SOFTWARE & ENTREPRENEURSHIP / MAY 2021–APR 2025",
    "title": "Software and automation at Bytes",
    "technical": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I co-founded Bytes.io in May 2021 and helped build the business from the ground up, shaping our service model, client acquisition, team roles, and day-to-day operations. As the company grew, I built the systems behind its operations: automating restaurant onboarding and menu generation, bringing six data sources into a shared database, generating performance reports, monitoring order data, and reconciling storefront records across platforms. These tools reduced manual work and supported a growing client base, with analytics covering more than 2,000 storefronts. My work with Bytes continued through April 2025.</p><section class=\"bytes-explainer\" aria-labelledby=\"bytes-explainer-title\"><h3 id=\"bytes-explainer-title\">What Bytes does</h3><p>Bytes helps brick-and-mortar restaurants reach more customers through online delivery platforms. We turn categories from an existing restaurant’s menu into focused virtual brands, each with its own name, menu, and product presentation. A kitchen serving a broad menu can offer separate storefronts for breakfast burritos, pizza, or chicken using the food it already prepares.</p><p>Customers order from those storefronts, and the same restaurant kitchen prepares the food. Bytes supports the storefront setup and operations; the software I built made menu creation, reporting, and reconciliation easier to manage as the network grew.</p><div class=\"bytes-explainer-images\"><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/bytes-virtual-brands.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/bytes-virtual-brands.jpg\" alt=\"Bytes explainer showing several focused virtual restaurant brands created from one restaurant’s menu\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1440\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>One kitchen, multiple online storefronts.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/bytes-menu-presentation.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"assets/bytes-menu-presentation.jpg\" alt=\"Bytes example comparing a focused branded menu with the original restaurant menu on a delivery platform\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1440\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Focused menus with clear product photos.</figcaption></figure></div></section><h3>Tools and skills</h3><div class=\"detail-skills\"><div><h4>Software and AI</h4><p>Python, GCP, GPT-3.5 prompting, inventory parsing, and automated menu generation.</p></div><div><h4>Data infrastructure</h4><p>PostgreSQL on AWS RDS, relational data modeling, Pandas ETL, and AWS Lambda.</p></div><div><h4>Automation and monitoring</h4><p>EventBridge triggers, order-data checks, Colab reporting, and report distribution.</p></div><div><h4>Record matching</h4><p>Jaccard similarity, cross-system reconciliation, and low-confidence match review.</p></div></div><h3>1. AI-assisted menu creation</h3><p>In 2023, I developed a Python and GCP workflow that parsed restaurant inventory and used GPT-3.5 prompting to generate themed virtual concepts. The tool reduced onboarding time by 97% and generated more than 1,000 storefronts, converting a manual operational bottleneck into a repeatable software pipeline.</p><h3>2. Centralized data and reporting</h3><p>I designed a relational PostgreSQL system on AWS RDS that unified six fragmented data sources. Python and Pandas ETL jobs running through Lambda automated data processing and supported analytics for more than 2,000 storefronts.</p><p>I also built Colab tools for querying store-level metrics, distributing reports, and sending region-wide updates. EventBridge and Lambda monitoring checked critical order data so operational issues could be detected sooner.</p><h3>3. Financial reconciliation</h3><p>I built a reconciliation tool that matched storefront records across marketplaces, databases, and order systems using Jaccard similarity. Low-confidence matches were routed for human review, reducing audit time by more than 90% without hiding ambiguous records.</p><a class=\"button\" href=\"https://trybytes.ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Visit Bytes ↗</a>",
    "storyTitle": "Co-founding and building Bytes",
    "story": "<p class=\"detail-lead\">I co-founded Bytes in May 2021 and helped take it from an early idea to an operating restaurant-technology platform through April 2025.</p><h3>My contributions as a co-founder</h3><p>As a co-founder, I worked across the full path from problem definition to deployed systems. I translated operational needs into software, built the data foundation, automated high-volume workflows, and maintained the tools that the business relied on as it grew.</p><h3>Hiring and building the team</h3><p>Once we had our first ten clients, the next challenge was giving the company room to grow. I defined roles, documented how the work got done, and hired our first employee. I remember feeling the weight of that decision: someone else was choosing to build with us. It pushed me to think more carefully about how I communicated expectations and supported the people doing the work.</p><p>As those early hires became a team, I learned to pay attention to what each person brought to it. I wanted people to have a clear part of the company they could shape and feel proud of. Working through decisions together and seeing colleagues take pride in our progress became some of the most meaningful parts of building Bytes. That experience shaped how I approach engineering teams: make the work understandable, listen to different perspectives, and give people room to take ownership.</p><h3>Representing Bytes</h3><p>As a cofounder, I represented Bytes at networking events, built relationships with restaurant operators, and introduced prospective clients and partners to our service model.</p><div class=\"bytes-events-gallery\"><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/bytes-networking.jpg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Hashem talking with attendees at a Bytes networking event\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/bytes-networking.jpg\"/></a><figcaption>At a Bytes networking event.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"detail-figure\"><a href=\"assets/bytes-delicious-booth.jpg\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"Delicious Bytes event booth with branded backdrop, restaurant-growth banner, and printed materials\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/bytes-delicious-booth.jpg\"/></a><figcaption>Delicious Bytes booth and event materials.</figcaption></figure></div><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"https://trybytes.ai/\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Visit Bytes ↗</a><h3>Bytes team and events</h3><figure class=\"detail-figure detail-video\"><video aria-label=\"Silent video from Bytes\" autoplay=\"\" loop=\"\" muted=\"\" playsinline=\"\" poster=\"assets/bytes-video.jpg\" preload=\"metadata\"><source src=\"assets/bytes-video.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"/>Your browser does not support this video.</video><figcaption>A short look at Bytes. <button class=\"text-link\" data-video-toggle=\"\" type=\"button\">Pause video</button></figcaption></figure>"
}
};

